# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from _e38_gen import t, body, MS, PH, AREA, DOD_DESIGN

D = dict(kind="Task", component="web", phase=PH, milestone=MS, perspective="Product", parent="E38")

t(**D, key="E38-D01", title="UX research: how traders tell demo from live and trust simulated fills",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p1", "design", "ux-research"], sprint="Sprint 16",
  priority="P1 High", risk="R9 Advice boundary", estimate=2, blocked_by=["E30", "E29"],
  body=body(
   ctx="E38 ships the two surfaces where a mistake is unrecoverable: the environment switch (SCR-074) and the permanent demo/live indication (SCR-075), plus three surfaces where a *misread* is nearly as damaging - the simulated-fill results drawer (SCR-099), the parity report (SCR-137) and the paper-balance reset (SCR-079).\n\n`docs/plan/11-user-stories.md` section 21 US-PAPER-002 demands that switching is 'deliberate and unmistakable'; US-PAPER-004 demands that simulated positions are 'visually marked as simulated everywhere'; US-PAPER-005 demands paper results never aggregate into live statistics. Those are research questions before they are visual questions: what do users actually look at before pressing submit, and do they discount simulated results appropriately or over-trust them?\n\nThis ticket is the research pass that de-risks D02-D04 and runs two sprints ahead of the consuming stories (design-ahead rule, `docs/plan/00-planning-brief.md` Team & cadence).",
   scope="- Moderated sessions with the Owner and 2 account managers (personas `docs/plan/10-personas.md` owner-trader, account manager), 45 min each, run against the R2 build plus clickable stubs.\n- Research questions:\n  1. Before submitting an order, which chrome element (if any) is consulted to confirm the environment? Test by asking participants to state the environment at unprompted moments.\n  2. Does a single global band (CMP-074 EnvBanner) suffice, or is the per-panel badge (CMP-075 EnvBadgeLocal) load-bearing when a panel is floated/maximised?\n  3. What confirmation friction is acceptable on the demo-to-live switch (typed phrase + step-up per SCR-074) before users start defeating it?\n  4. How do users interpret simulated P&L from the paper matcher - do they treat it as achievable, and what framing (per SCR-099's 'simulated' checklist) corrects over-trust?\n  5. What do users expect 'reset paper balance' (US-PAPER-006) to destroy, and do they expect live data to be touched?\n  6. Would a parity report (US-PAPER-008) be read proactively or only after a surprise, and what does 'last verified' need to say to be trusted?\n- Greyscale/colour-vision pre-test of demo vs live differentiation (deuteranopia, protanopia, tritanopia simulations) - required because `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-075 forbids colour-only encoding.\n- Deliverable: research report in `docs/design/research/E38-paper-parity.md` with findings, verbatims, a prioritised implications list mapped to SCR-074/075/079/099/122/137, and any risk raised into `docs/plan/32-risk-register.md`.",
   oos="- Visual design of any screen (that is D02-D04).\n- Rule-editor, order-ticket or DOM research (E30/E31/E36/E37 own theirs).\n- Quantitative A/B testing - the population is 3-5 users; findings are qualitative by design.",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Research executed and reported\n  Given 3 participants covering the owner and manager personas\n  When all sessions are complete\n  Then a report exists at docs/design/research/E38-paper-parity.md answering all six research questions with evidence\n\nScenario: Environment-recall finding is measured, not asserted\n  Given each participant is interrupted at 3 unprompted moments per session\n  When asked to state the current trading environment\n  Then the recall rate is recorded per chrome treatment and drives an explicit recommendation on whether per-panel badges are mandatory\n\nScenario: Colour-independence validated\n  Given the candidate demo and live treatments\n  When each is rendered in greyscale and in the three colour-vision simulations\n  Then any treatment that becomes ambiguous is rejected in the report and not carried into D02\n\nScenario: Over-trust of simulated results surfaced\n  Given participants are shown a simulated results drawer with a strong equity curve\n  When asked what they would size their next live trade at\n  Then any evidence of over-trust is recorded and converted into a concrete framing requirement for SCR-099\n```",
   tech="- Sessions recorded (with consent) and stored on the project's private research drive, not in the repo; the repo holds only the anonymised report.\n- Stimuli: R2 build with a Figma-prototype overlay for the switch modal; do not use production credentials - use `CV_ENV=dev` seeds (`docs/plan/21-database-schema.md` seeds section) so no real key or balance is on screen.\n- Colour-vision simulation via a Figma/Stark plugin against the `color.env.demo` / `color.env.live` tokens from `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`.\n- Report template per question: method, evidence, finding, confidence, implication, owning ticket.",
   test="- N/A (research, not code). Validation is peer review of the report by the Chief Design Officer and the Architect.\n- Findings that make testable claims (e.g. 'badge must repeat per panel') become acceptance criteria on D02 and QA scenarios in E38-Q01.",
   sec="- Threat: research recordings could capture API key material or real balances. Control: run against `CV_ENV=dev` seed data only (fake encrypted keys per `docs/plan/21-database-schema.md`); no live or funded demo account on screen. Data classification: internal; recordings confidential and not committed.\n- No `security` review label required.",
   a11y="- Colour-vision and greyscale validation is an explicit deliverable (WCAG 2.2 AA 1.4.1 Use of Colour, `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md`).\n- At least one session exercises keyboard-only operation of the switch flow to check the confirmation gate is not pointer-dependent.",
   perf="N/A - no runtime component.",
   obs="N/A - no runtime component. Findings that imply telemetry (e.g. 'count environment mis-reads') are filed as follow-up tickets against E38-T02.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by: E30` (order-ticket design exists, so the badge context is real) and `E29` (OMS order flow defined) - participants must react to a realistic trading surface, not a blank page.",
   branch="`design/e38-paper-research` (report only); PR size: docs-only, single report file.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/11-user-stories.md` section 21 PAPER (US-PAPER-002, 004, 005, 006, 008)\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-074, SCR-075, SCR-079, SCR-099, SCR-122, SCR-137\n- `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md` CMP-074, CMP-075, CMP-139\n- `docs/plan/10-personas.md`, `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md`, `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`"))

t(**D, key="E38-D02", title="Hi-fi design: environment switcher SCR-074 and demo/live chrome SCR-075",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p0", "design"], sprint="Sprint 16",
  priority="P0 Critical", risk="R5 Scope", estimate=5, blocked_by=["E38-D01"],
  body=body(
   ctx="SCR-075 is described in `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` as 'the single most safety-critical visual element in the app'. SCR-074 is the only way to reach live, and per `docs/plan/22-api-openapi.yaml` `POST /session/environment` it requires an elevated session plus the `trading.live_enabled` flag. These two must be designed together: the switcher is a moment, the chrome is the permanent consequence.\n\nWireframes already exist in the screens catalogue; this ticket takes them to signed-off hi-fi with every state drawn, so the consuming story E38-S04 start with zero open design questions.",
   scope="- Hi-fi Figma frames for **SCR-074 Environment switcher (Demo to Live)**, all states from the catalogue: idle, switching (progress: closing subscriptions, re-authenticating, resubscribing), switched (10 s full-width confirmation banner), failed (stays on previous environment with reason), not permitted ('The owner hasn't enabled Live for your account.'), blocked (an algo or trade group is running).\n- Hi-fi frames for **SCR-075 Demo / Live banner & per-panel badges**: global band in both environments; per-panel badge placement drawn for order ticket, group ticket, DOM ladder, positions grid, algo monitor and chart-trading overlay; floating-window repetition; behaviour under a modal (the band must never be covered).\n- Demo copy variant on SCR-075 stating 'Demo uses Bybit's demo matching engine - REST order entry only, no private WS order entry.'\n- Design-system contributions: finalise **CMP-074 EnvBanner** and **CMP-075 EnvBadgeLocal** specs (tokens `color.env.demo`, `color.env.live`, `font.weight.bold`) and the disabled-with-reason presentation used by **CMP-139 EnvironmentAwareOrderGuard**; raise any new token as a design-system ticket rather than a one-off.\n- Motion spec: the chrome transition during a switch (duration, easing, reduced-motion fallback) per `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`.\n- Redlines and a state-machine diagram of the switch (idle, confirming, switching, switched | failed | not-permitted).",
   oos="- The step-up/2FA modal itself (owned by E09 auth).\n- Order-ticket internals (E30) - this ticket specifies only where the badge sits in it.\n- The parity report and paper-fidelity surfaces (E38-D03).",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Every catalogued state is drawn\n  Given the SCR-074 and SCR-075 entries in docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md\n  When the Figma file is reviewed\n  Then every listed state exists as a named frame and the screens' design sign-off checklists are fully ticked\n\nScenario: Colour is never the only signal\n  Given the demo and live treatments\n  When rendered in greyscale and in three colour-vision simulations\n  Then DEMO and LIVE remain unambiguous through text plus icon plus pattern\n\nScenario: The switch cannot be hit accidentally\n  Given the SCR-074 design\n  When a reviewer attempts to find a single-click or hotkey path from demo to live\n  Then none exists: the flow requires a typed confirmation phrase plus step-up, and the design annotates that no hotkey may be bound\n\nScenario: Blocked switch is designed, not left to engineering\n  Given a running emulated algo or an in-flight trade group\n  When the user opens SCR-074\n  Then the switch action is disabled with the specific blocking reason listed, and the frame exists\n\nScenario: The band survives a modal\n  Given any modal dialog is open over the app shell\n  When the frames are reviewed\n  Then the environment band remains visible and is annotated as never scrolled away or covered\n```",
   tech="- Frames follow the app-shell grid from SCR-010 so the band's height is budgeted in the shell layout, not added later.\n- Annotate the accessible-name contract: band is `role=\"status\"`, read early in document order; each trading panel's accessible name includes the environment ('Order ticket - LIVE - Main') per SCR-075; CMP-075 is `role=\"img\"` with a static label to avoid re-announcement storms.\n- Annotate the 10 s post-switch confirmation banner as `role=\"alert\"` (assertive), announced once.\n- Reduced-motion: the chrome transition degrades to an instant swap under `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Provide both themes (light/dark) and both density settings for every frame.",
   test="- Design review walkthrough with the Architect and the Security engineer (the switch is a security control, not just UI).\n- Accessibility annotation review is E38-D05; design QA against the built screens is E38-D07.\n- Contrast measured for every env treatment against `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md` thresholds (>=4.5:1 text, >=3:1 non-text) and recorded in the file.",
   sec="- This design is the user-facing half of security requirement **SR-040a** (environment capability matrix enforced in code) and **SR-043** (armed state for live). The design must not imply any capability the backend forbids - notably it must state demo's REST-only order entry rather than hiding it.\n- Threat (STRIDE spoofing/tampering): a user believing they are in demo while routed to live. Mitigation is this design plus the server-side gate in E38-T02; Security reviews before sign-off.\n- Data classification: none rendered (no keys, no PII).",
   a11y="- WCAG 2.2 AA: 1.4.1 (colour not sole means), 1.4.3/1.4.11 (contrast), 2.1.1 (keyboard), 2.4.3 (focus order: the typed-confirm field receives focus on open), 4.1.3 (status messages).\n- The typed confirmation field must have a visible label and an announced error state, not colour-only.\n- Annotate focus return: on cancel, focus returns to the env badge that opened the modal.",
   perf="- Annotate that the band is part of the app shell and must not remount on route change (SCR-075 performance note); badge state comes from one shared store subscription.\n- The switch transition blocks order entry for the whole transition (typically <=2 s) - the design shows that blocked state rather than an optimistic UI.",
   obs="- Annotate which interaction emits `session.environment_switched` (high-severity audit event per SCR-074) so engineering wires the audit trigger at the right point (on success, not on intent).",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by: E38-D01` for the recall and colour-vision findings.\n- Relies on the app-shell grid (SCR-010) from R1 layout work and on the step-up modal pattern owned by E09.",
   branch="`design/e38-env-chrome` (Figma link + catalogue updates); PR size: docs-only.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-074, SCR-075, SCR-010\n- `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md` CMP-074, CMP-075, CMP-139, CMP-044\n- `docs/plan/04-security-program.md` SR-040a, SR-043\n- `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`, `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md`"))

t(**D, key="E38-D03", title="Hi-fi design: parity report, paper fidelity panel, reset and eligibility",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p1", "design"], sprint="Sprint 17",
  priority="P1 High", risk="R5 Scope", estimate=3, blocked_by=["E38-D01"],
  body=body(
   ctx="Three smaller surfaces round out E38 and all are 'honesty' surfaces: the environment parity report (US-PAPER-008, on SCR-137 Admin security centre and linked from SCR-074), the admin **paper fidelity** panel showing the nightly paper-vs-demo divergence metrics (`docs/plan/24-internal-schemas.md` section 12.5), the paper-balance reset on SCR-079 (US-PAPER-006), and the demo/live eligibility control on SCR-122 (US-PAPER-007).\n\nThey are grouped into one design ticket because they share a pattern language (admin tables, destructive confirmation, disabled-with-reason) and the same reviewer, and none is big enough to justify its own.",
   scope="- **SCR-137 Admin: security centre** - an 'Environment parity' section: table of known demo-vs-live differences with columns difference, area, source, last verified, status; a 'needs re-verification' treatment for items older than 90 days; a 'candidate parity item' inbox fed by runtime-detected demo-only failures. Components: CMP-028, CMP-036, CMP-050, CMP-087, CMP-093, CMP-175, CMP-178.\n- **Admin paper fidelity panel** - the nightly divergence report: mean and p95 slippage difference, fill-rate difference for resting limits, fee difference, with the defect threshold (p95 slippage >1 tick or fill-rate >10 pp) drawn as an explicit breach state. Reuses CMP-178 SystemHealthTile and CMP-049 Table.\n- **SCR-079 Account detail drawer (trading side)** - the 'Reset paper balance' control: starting-balance input, the warning when open paper positions exist, the explicit confirmation, and the **hidden-entirely-in-live** treatment (US-PAPER-006 requires hidden, not merely disabled).\n- **SCR-122 Admin: user detail / edit** - demo/live eligibility control: demo-only vs live-enabled, the reason shown when Live is disabled for a manager, the acknowledgement-at-next-sign-in pattern on promotion, and the reduce-only state after revocation while positions are open.\n- Empty, loading, error and RBAC-denied states for each.\n- Design-system: reuse only; any new pattern raised as a contribution ticket.",
   oos="- The switcher and global chrome (E38-D02).\n- Replay simulated-results drawer (E38-D04).\n- The wider admin screens (E42 owns SCR-122's and SCR-137's overall structure; this ticket designs only the E38 sections inside them).",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Parity report communicates freshness\n  Given a parity item last verified 95 days ago\n  When the SCR-137 parity section is reviewed\n  Then a distinct 'needs re-verification' state is drawn, using text plus icon, not colour alone\n\nScenario: Reset is hidden in live\n  Given the environment is LIVE\n  When the SCR-079 frames are reviewed\n  Then the reset control is absent from the drawer entirely, and a note states it must not render as a disabled control\n\nScenario: Destructive reset is guarded\n  Given open paper positions exist\n  When the user initiates a reset\n  Then a confirmation is drawn that names the count of positions and orders to be discarded and requires an explicit confirm\n\nScenario: Revocation with open positions is designed\n  Given a manager's live access is revoked while they hold live positions\n  When the SCR-122 frames are reviewed\n  Then a reduce-only state is drawn with copy explaining existing positions remain manageable and new entries are refused\n\nScenario: Fidelity breach is unmissable\n  Given the nightly divergence job reports p95 slippage divergence above one tick\n  When the fidelity panel is reviewed\n  Then a breach state is drawn that states the threshold, the measured value and the resulting action (a defect ticket is opened)\n```",
   tech="- Table density and sorting follow the admin table conventions set by E42's SCR-120..SCR-140 work; do not invent a new table.\n- Parity items are data-driven (US-PAPER-008 NFR: 'generated from a maintained data file plus runtime observations, never hand-written prose only') - the design must accommodate a variable-length, machine-generated list including items with missing 'source' values.\n- The reset confirmation uses CMP-044 ConfirmDialog; specify which variant (typed vs plain) and justify it.\n- Annotate audit triggers: reset writes to the paper journal and an audit row; eligibility grant/revoke is audited (US-PAPER-007).",
   test="- Review walkthrough with the Owner (these are owner-facing admin surfaces) and the Architect.\n- Accessibility annotation review in E38-D05; design QA in E38-D07.",
   sec="- SCR-122 eligibility is a privilege control: the design must make the current grant state unambiguous and must not offer a path for a manager to self-grant. Reviewed against `docs/plan/04-security-program.md` section 7 RBAC 4-tuple.\n- SCR-137 is a security surface; no secret or key material may appear in any frame (keys are never readable back, per the locked decisions).\n- Data classification: admin/operational metadata; no PII beyond usernames.",
   a11y="- Tables need real table semantics with header associations; 'needs re-verification' and 'breach' states must carry text, not only icon colour.\n- Destructive confirm: focus moves into the dialog, the confirm button is not the default focus, and the dialog is labelled by its heading.\n- Reduce-only and disabled-with-reason states follow the CMP-079 RbacGate pattern so the reason is programmatically associated with the control.",
   perf="- The parity table is small (tens of rows) - no virtualisation needed; annotate that the fidelity panel loads a single pre-aggregated nightly document rather than querying raw executions.",
   obs="- Annotate the audit events the surfaces trigger: paper reset, eligibility grant/revoke, parity item verification.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by: E38-D01`. Sits inside screens whose shells are owned by E42 (SCR-122, SCR-137) and E27/E28 (SCR-079) - coordinate frame ownership rather than forking those files.",
   branch="`design/e38-parity-admin`; PR size: docs-only.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/11-user-stories.md` US-PAPER-006, US-PAPER-007, US-PAPER-008\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-079, SCR-122, SCR-137\n- `docs/plan/24-internal-schemas.md` section 12.5 divergence checking\n- `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md` CMP-028, CMP-036, CMP-044, CMP-049, CMP-050, CMP-087, CMP-093, CMP-175, CMP-178"))

t(**D, key="E38-D04", title="Hi-fi design: simulated-fill results SCR-099 and simulated-everywhere labelling",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p1", "design"], sprint="Sprint 17",
  priority="P1 High", risk="R12 Backtest bias", estimate=3, blocked_by=["E38-D01"],
  body=body(
   ctx="US-PAPER-004 requires that simulated positions are 'visually marked as simulated everywhere', and US-PAPER-005 requires that paper results 'never aggregate into live statistics, with a clear label on every view'. That is not one screen - it is a cross-cutting labelling system riding on the `is_paper` boolean (`docs/plan/21-database-schema.md` section 3.3, `docs/plan/23-ws-protocol.md` section 13.1).\n\nThis ticket designs SCR-099 (Replay paper-trading results) and the `is_paper` labelling system that every blotter, position row, journal entry and analytics view must adopt.",
   scope="- Hi-fi frames for **SCR-099 Replay paper-trading results** per its catalogue entry: simulated fills, P&L, R distribution, the modelled slippage assumptions, the explicit 'these are simulated' statement, per-trade list with entry/exit and R, opt-in comparison against the same period's real trades (clearly separated), export, and the 'not a backtest guarantee' caveat. Components: CMP-049 Table, CMP-099 PrintExportBar, CMP-116 PnLBadge, CMP-167 JournalEquityCurveChart, CMP-168 JournalStatsSummaryCard.\n- A **fill-model disclosure block** design: queue position, latency (submit/cancel/market-data), slippage mode, fee source, funding applied - readable by a non-engineer, sourced from `FillModelConfig` (`docs/plan/24-internal-schemas.md` section 12.1).\n- The **simulated labelling system**: one badge spec plus placement rules for the orders blotter, positions grid, journal list, journal trade detail and analytics headers; rule that the badge appears in the heading, not only as a chip (SCR-099 a11y note); rule for how analytics excludes simulated rows by default and how the include-toggle is presented.\n- Table alternatives for every chart on SCR-099 (a11y requirement from the catalogue entry).\n- Design-system contribution: the simulated badge as a catalogue entry rather than a page-local element.",
   oos="- Journal analytics screens themselves (E41) - this ticket contributes the simulated treatment they must adopt.\n- Replay transport/scrubbing UI (E26).\n- The environment badge (that is D02; environment and simulated are orthogonal and must be visually distinguishable).",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Simulated framing is inescapable\n  Given any frame showing paper-matcher output\n  When reviewed\n  Then the word 'Simulated' appears in the region heading and in the accessible name, not only as a coloured chip\n\nScenario: Environment and simulated are not confused\n  Given a demo-environment simulated fill\n  When the frames are reviewed\n  Then the DEMO environment badge and the SIMULATED badge are visually and textually distinct, and a note explains they are orthogonal\n\nScenario: Fill assumptions are stated, not buried\n  Given the SCR-099 results drawer\n  When a user opens it for the first time\n  Then the queue-position, latency, slippage and fee assumptions are visible without expanding a disclosure, and each names its configured value\n\nScenario: Every chart has a table alternative\n  Given the equity curve and R-distribution visualisations\n  When the frames are reviewed\n  Then a table alternative is drawn for each and is reachable by keyboard\n\nScenario: Comparison against real trades is opt-in and separated\n  Given the comparison feature\n  When reviewed\n  Then it is off by default and the simulated and real series are separated visually and in the legend, never merged into one total\n```",
   tech="- Visual encodings follow the project's data-visualisation conventions: categorical series distinguishable without colour, direct labelling preferred over legends for two-series comparisons.\n- Numeric formatting uses the mono token and the instrument's tick/lot precision from `instruments` (`docs/plan/21-database-schema.md`).\n- Annotate that results are computed server-side at session end and fetched once (SCR-099 performance note) - so the design has a loading state and no live-updating figures.\n- Specify the export affordance (CMP-099) and its formats (CSV/Parquet, matching E41's export).",
   test="- Review with the Owner focused specifically on over-trust: show the frames and ask what the numbers mean (carrying forward the E38-D01 finding).\n- Accessibility annotation review in E38-D05; design QA in E38-D07.",
   sec="- Threat: simulated results promoted into live analytics and used to justify real risk (R12 backtest bias). Control: designed-in separation plus the `simulated=true` journal flag audited as `journal.simulated_import` (SCR-099 analytics/audit note).\n- Data classification: trading performance data - internal, not public; the export affordance must not offer an unauthenticated share link.",
   a11y="- 'Simulated' must be in the heading and in each region's accessible name (SCR-099 a11y note).\n- Every chart needs a table alternative; results are a table plus a summary description list.\n- Export controls need accessible names stating format and scope ('Export 42 simulated trades as CSV').",
   perf="- Single fetch at session end; no open subscriptions remain. Annotate a skeleton loading state sized to the final layout to avoid layout shift.",
   obs="- Annotate `replay.results_viewed`, `replay.results_exported`, `replay.results_promoted_to_journal` triggers so engineering places them precisely.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by: E38-D01`. Depends on E26 replay session semantics and E41 journal visual language; coordinate the badge contribution with the design-system team.",
   branch="`design/e38-simulated-results`; PR size: docs-only.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/11-user-stories.md` US-PAPER-004, US-PAPER-005, US-RPL-006\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-099\n- `docs/plan/24-internal-schemas.md` section 12 paper matcher\n- `docs/plan/23-ws-protocol.md` section 13.1 `is_paper`"))

t(**D, key="E38-D05", title="Accessibility design review of every E38 surface",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p1", "design", "a11y"], sprint="Sprint 17",
  priority="P1 High", risk="R5 Scope", estimate=2, blocked_by=["E38-D02", "E38-D03", "E38-D04"],
  body=body(
   ctx="`docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md` makes WCAG 2.2 AA mandatory for every UI surface including owner/admin screens, and `docs/plan/02-definition-of-ready-done.md` section 8 states there is no 'internal tool' exception. E38's surfaces carry an unusually heavy a11y load: the environment distinction must survive colour blindness and greyscale, the switch is a destructive-class action requiring announced confirmation, and SCR-099 charts each need a table alternative.\n\nThis is the pre-build accessibility gate on the designs from D02-D04, done before handoff so engineering never implements an inaccessible spec.",
   scope="- Review SCR-074, SCR-075, SCR-079 (reset), SCR-099, SCR-122 (eligibility) and SCR-137 (parity + fidelity) frames against `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md`.\n- Produce, per screen, an annotation layer covering: focus order, initial focus, focus return on dismiss, accessible names for every control, live-region roles and politeness, heading hierarchy, keyboard operation of every interaction, and error-message association.\n- Verify colour-independence and contrast measurements for `color.env.demo`/`color.env.live` and for the simulated badge; record measured ratios.\n- Confirm reduced-motion fallbacks for the environment-switch transition.\n- Deliver a defect list against D02/D03/D04 (Figma comments plus a checklist on this ticket); blocking items must be fixed before D06 handoff.",
   oos="- Automated axe-core runs against built code (E38-Q05).\n- Manual screen-reader testing of the shipped build (also E38-Q05).\n- Remediation of non-E38 screens the review happens to pass through.",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Every interactive control has a specified accessible name\n  Given the six reviewed screens\n  When the annotation layer is complete\n  Then every control has an explicit accessible-name annotation and none is left to engineering's judgement\n\nScenario: Contrast is measured, not eyeballed\n  Given the environment and simulated treatments in both themes\n  When measured\n  Then every text pairing is at least 4.5:1 and every non-text indicator at least 3:1, with the measured value recorded per pairing\n\nScenario: A failure blocks handoff\n  Given a blocking accessibility defect is found in a D02/D03/D04 frame\n  When E38-D06 handoff is attempted\n  Then handoff is refused until the defect is fixed and re-reviewed\n\nScenario: Keyboard-only path exists for the environment switch\n  Given a keyboard-only user\n  When the annotated switch flow is traced\n  Then the full path from badge to typed confirmation to step-up to completion is operable without a pointer, with visible focus at each step\n```",
   tech="- Use the annotation conventions in `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md` (focus-order numbering, live-region tags, name/role/value callouts).\n- Special attention to announcement storms: CMP-075 repeats on every trading panel; the spec must prevent every panel re-announcing the environment on each render (`role=\"img\"` with a static label per the component catalogue).\n- The post-switch banner is assertive and announced once; the band itself is `role=\"status\"` and polite.",
   test="- Peer review by the accessibility specialist plus one screen-reader walkthrough of the annotated prototype (NVDA on Windows, the primary target since the shell is Electron on Windows).\n- Output feeds the E38-Q05 audit scenarios so the audit tests exactly what was specified.",
   sec="- No threats of its own; note only that the not-permitted state on SCR-074 must not leak whether other users have live access (avoid information disclosure in the reason copy).",
   a11y="- This ticket *is* the accessibility work: WCAG 2.2 AA across 1.3.1, 1.4.1, 1.4.3, 1.4.11, 2.1.1, 2.4.3, 2.4.7, 2.4.11 (focus not obscured), 3.3.1, 3.3.2, 4.1.2, 4.1.3.",
   perf="- Verify the announcement strategy does not force synchronous re-renders of every trading panel on environment change (cross-check with the shared-store approach in SCR-075's performance note).",
   obs="N/A.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by`: D02, D03, D04 must be at review-ready fidelity.",
   branch="`design/e38-a11y-review`; PR size: docs-only.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/05-accessibility-standard.md`\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` SCR-074, SCR-075, SCR-079, SCR-099, SCR-122, SCR-137\n- `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md` CMP-074, CMP-075, CMP-139"))

t(**D, key="E38-D06", title="Design handoff package for the E38 surfaces",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p0", "design", "handoff"], sprint="Sprint 17",
  priority="P0 Critical", risk="R5 Scope", estimate=2, blocked_by=["E38-D05"],
  body=body(
   ctx="The design-ahead rule (`docs/plan/00-planning-brief.md`) forbids frontend work starting before its design ticket is Done, and `docs/plan/02-definition-of-ready-done.md` section 3.1 requires the design to have been Done for at least 2 sprints before the consuming story. E38's frontend stories run in Sprint 18-19, so handoff must complete in Sprint 17.\n\nThis ticket packages D02-D05 into something an engineer can build from without asking a question.",
   scope="- A single handoff document `docs/design/handoff/E38.md` linking, per screen (SCR-074, SCR-075, SCR-079 reset, SCR-099, SCR-122 eligibility, SCR-137 parity + fidelity): Figma frame URLs, state inventory, redlines, token list, motion spec, a11y annotation layer, and copy deck (exact strings, including the demo REST-only notice and the 'not a backtest guarantee' caveat).\n- Component mapping table: which catalogue components (CMP-074, CMP-075, CMP-139, CMP-044, CMP-049, CMP-099, CMP-116, CMP-167, CMP-168, CMP-175, CMP-178) each screen composes, and which need new variants.\n- Asset export: icons/patterns used for the colour-independent environment encoding.\n- Walkthrough session with the frontend engineers who will build E38-S03, S04, S05, S06, S07 and S08, recorded, with an open-questions log resolved before the ticket closes.\n- Update `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md` and `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md` where the final design diverged from the written entry.",
   oos="- Building anything.\n- Design QA of the built result (E38-D07).",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Handoff is self-sufficient\n  Given an engineer who has not attended any design review\n  When they read docs/design/handoff/E38.md\n  Then they can identify every state, token, string and interaction for each screen without asking a designer\n\nScenario: Copy is final, not lorem\n  Given the copy deck\n  When reviewed\n  Then every user-visible string is final and approved, including error and not-permitted copy\n\nScenario: Open questions are closed\n  Given the walkthrough session's open-questions log\n  When this ticket moves to Done\n  Then every entry has a recorded resolution or an explicitly owned follow-up ticket\n\nScenario: Design-ahead satisfied\n  Given the consuming stories are scheduled for Sprint 18 and 19\n  When this ticket reaches Done\n  Then its Done date is at or before the end of Sprint 17, satisfying the design-ahead rule\n```",
   tech="- Token names must match `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md` exactly; no invented names in redlines.\n- The copy deck is the source for i18n keys even though v1 is English-only - key names are agreed here so strings are never hardcoded.\n- Include the environment-switch state-machine diagram so the frontend store models the same states the design assumes.",
   test="- Validation is the walkthrough: an engineer restates each screen's states from the package alone.\n- QA consumes this package to write E38-Q01, so a QA representative attends the walkthrough.",
   sec="- The copy deck is reviewed for information disclosure (the not-permitted reason must not reveal other users' grants) - Security signs off the copy deck sections covering SCR-074 and SCR-122.",
   a11y="- The a11y annotation layer from D05 is part of the package and is non-optional; the walkthrough explicitly covers the keyboard path and announcement behaviour.",
   perf="- The package restates the shell-level constraints: the band never remounts; the switch blocks order entry during transition; SCR-099 fetches once.",
   obs="- The package lists the audit/analytics events each surface triggers so engineering wires them during the story, not afterwards.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by: E38-D05` (accessibility gate must pass first).\n- Consumed by E38-S03, E38-S04, E38-S05, E38-S06, E38-S07 and E38-S08.",
   branch="`design/e38-handoff`; PR size: docs-only.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/00-planning-brief.md` Team & cadence (design-ahead)\n- `docs/plan/02-definition-of-ready-done.md` section 3.1\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md`, `docs/plan/15-component-catalogue.md`, `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`"))

t(**D, key="E38-D07", title="Design QA of the shipped E38 surfaces",
  labels=["type/design", AREA, "priority/p1", "design", "design-qa"], sprint="Sprint 19",
  priority="P1 High", risk="R5 Scope", estimate=2,
  blocked_by=["E38-S03", "E38-S04", "E38-S05", "E38-S06", "E38-S07", "E38-S08"],
  body=body(
   ctx="`docs/plan/02-definition-of-ready-done.md` section 3.2 makes design QA default-on for net-new UI stories. E38 ships six surfaces whose whole purpose is unambiguous communication; a spacing or contrast drift here is a safety defect, not a cosmetic one.",
   scope="- Compare the built SCR-074, SCR-075 (incl. per-panel badges on ticket, group ticket, DOM ladder, positions grid, algo monitor, chart overlay), SCR-079 reset, SCR-099, SCR-122 eligibility and SCR-137 parity + fidelity against the D06 handoff package.\n- Check per surface: spacing, type scale, token usage (no hardcoded colours), all states rendered, copy matching the deck verbatim, motion matching the spec including the reduced-motion fallback, both themes, both densities.\n- Verify the environment treatment in greyscale and colour-vision simulation on the **built** app, not just in Figma.\n- Verify the simulated badge appears on every surface the D04 placement rules list.\n- File discrepancies as Bug tickets with `priority/*` set, linked to this ticket and to the originating story.",
   oos="- Functional testing (E38-Q02/Q06) and accessibility auditing (E38-Q05) - overlaps are reported rather than ignored.\n- Redesign; if a discrepancy reveals a design gap, a new design ticket is filed.",
   ac="```gherkin\nScenario: Every handoff surface is reviewed\n  Given the six E38 surfaces\n  When design QA completes\n  Then each has a recorded pass or a filed discrepancy list, with no surface unreviewed\n\nScenario: Token drift is caught\n  Given the built environment band\n  When inspected in the browser\n  Then its colours resolve to color.env.demo / color.env.live tokens and not to literal hex values\n\nScenario: Copy drift is caught\n  Given the copy deck\n  When compared string-by-string against the build\n  Then any divergence is filed as a Bug, including punctuation and capitalisation of DEMO and LIVE\n\nScenario: Blocking discrepancy stops Done\n  Given a P0 or P1 discrepancy on a safety-critical surface (SCR-074 or SCR-075)\n  When the epic's Done is attempted\n  Then it is blocked until that discrepancy is fixed and re-verified\n```",
   tech="- Review on the staging (demo) deployment in both the web build and the Electron shell - the band must behave identically in both, including in a floating window.\n- Use the browser inspector to verify computed styles against tokens rather than relying on screenshots.\n- Capture before/after evidence on every filed discrepancy.",
   test="- This ticket is itself a verification activity; its output is a pass/fail record plus Bug tickets.\n- Re-verification of fixes is part of this ticket, not a new one.",
   sec="- If design QA finds a surface where the environment is ambiguous or absent, it is filed as `priority/p0-critical` with the `security` label - an unlabelled environment surface defeats SR-040a's user-facing half.",
   a11y="- Contrast is re-measured on the built app; any regression from the D05 measurements is filed with the `a11y` label.",
   perf="- Confirm the band does not remount on route change (React DevTools profiler) and that no per-panel badge triggers a full panel re-render on environment change.",
   obs="- Confirm the audit/analytics events listed in the handoff package actually fire, by inspecting the audit log on staging after exercising each surface.",
   dod=DOD_DESIGN,
   deps="- `blocked_by`: all E38 frontend stories must be merged and deployed to staging.",
   branch="`design/e38-design-qa` (findings doc); PR size: docs-only plus linked Bug tickets.",
   refs="- `docs/plan/02-definition-of-ready-done.md` section 3.2\n- `docs/design/handoff/E38.md` (produced by E38-D06)\n- `docs/plan/14-screens-catalogue.md`, `docs/plan/16-design-system-brief.md`"))
